  "prompt": "=== Chain-of-thought analysis prompt ===\nYou are a software requirements analyst preparing a UML state machine model.\n\nAnalyze the requirement and extract only the modeling facts needed for a state transition diagram. Keep the analysis concise and evidence-based.\n\nReturn the analysis using exactly these headings:\nStates:\nInitial state:\nFinal states:\nEvents or conditions:\nTransitions:\nMissing or ambiguous details:\n\nDo not write PlantUML in this step.\n\nRequirement:\nE Visa Processing And Follow Up System — Polished Requirement Specification\n\nFunctional Requirements\n1. The system shall allow a user to create an account.\n2. The system shall allow a user to log in after they have created an account.\n3. The system shall allow a user to apply for a visa after they have logged in, make the payment and then the application is accepted .\n4. The system shall allow a user to view the status after they have applied for a visa.\n5. The system shall allow a user to check the status after they have viewed it.\n6. The system shall allow a user to cancel an application after they have logged in.\n7. The system shall allow a user to modify their details after they have logged in.\n8. The system shall allow a user to view their details after they have logged in.\n9. The system shall allow a user to view the purpose of the application after they have logged in, Based on the document check, the visa is either approved or rejected, and then the status is updated..\n10. The system shall allow a user to give feedback after they have checked the status.\n11. The system shall allow a user to log out after they have modified their details, viewed their details, given feedback, or completed a status update.\n\n=== Chain-of-thought analysis response ===\nStates:\n- Account Creation\n- Logged In\n- Visa Application Pending\n- Visa Approved\n- Visa Rejected\n- Status Viewed\n- Feedback Given\n\nInitial state:\nAccount Creation\n\nFinal states:\nVisa Approved\nVisa Rejected\n\nEvents or conditions:\n- Create Account\n- Log In\n- Apply For Visa (after logging in)\n- Make Payment (after applying for visa)\n- Accept Application (after making payment)\n- View Status (after applying for visa)\n- Check Status (after viewing status)\n- Cancel Application (after logging in)\n- Modify Details (after logging in)\n- View Details (after logging in)\n- View Purpose of Application (after logging in)\n- Give Feedback (after checking status)\n- Log Out\n\nTransitions:\n1. From Account Creation to Logged In - Create Account\n2. From Logged In to Visa Application Pending - Apply For Visa\n3. From Visa Application Pending to Visa Approved - Accept Application\n4. From Visa Application Pending to Visa Rejected - Accept Application\n5. From Logged In to Status Viewed - View Status\n6. From Status Viewed to Feedback Given - Check Status\n7. From Logged In to Cancel Application - Cancel Application\n8. From Logged In to Modify Details - Modify Details\n9. From Logged In to View Details - View Details\n10. From Logged In to View Purpose of Application - View Purpose of Application\n11. From Any State to Logged In - Log Out\n\nMissing or ambiguous details:\n- The exact sequence and conditions for transitioning from Visa Application Pending to either Visa Approved or Visa Rejected are not specified.\n- It is unclear if the user can transition back to a previous state after giving feedback or modifying their details.\n\n=== PlantUML generation prompt ===\nYou are a UML modeling expert.\n\nConvert the requirement and the structured analysis into one UML state transition diagram in PlantUML format.\n\nOutput Rules:\n- Generate ONLY valid PlantUML code.\n- Start with @startuml and end with @enduml.\n- Use [*] for exactly one initial transition.\n- Include at least one final transition to [*].\n- Use clear transition labels when requirement evidence exists.\n- Do not include explanations, analysis, or markdown fences.\n\nRequirement:\nE Visa Processing And